In the sun‑blasted grass of Stranglethorn Vale, Gnomerick the Warlock trudged through a thicket of thorny brambles, his tiny succubus, Lilith, perched on his shoulder. “We’ll finish the goblin raid in a flash,” he muttered, flicking a shadow bolt at a snarling goblin. Lilith hissed, “I’ll seduce the leader—no one will notice us.” As the goblin’s eyes glazed, Gnomerick’s health dipped, and Lilith’s thin frame trembled. A sudden burst of melee damage from a nearby hunter sent Lilith crashing to the ground, her shadow fading. Gnomerick cursed, “I can’t keep her alive with all this mana drain!” He sighed, realizing that while Lilith could dish out damage, she was fragile and easy to kill. He’d need a sturdier companion to survive the next wave of enemies.

**Answer**  
Warlocks often choose voidwalkers because they are tanky, generate threat, and survive longer with less mana. Succubuses deal more damage and offer stealth, but they’re fragile and die quickly, forcing warlocks to spend extra mana to keep them alive. Thus, for leveling and questing, the survivability of voidwalkers outweighs the raw damage of succubuses. [end of text]


